.TH mnnn-user 1  "mnnn-user 2.11.1" 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
mnnn\-user \- You as a user: your identity across your nodes, linking another node to it and revoking one
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmnnn user\fR [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-theme\-mode\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
You as a user: your identity across your nodes, linking another node to it and revoking one
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Muninn home directory (default ~/.muninn)
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_HOME\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Database file (default <home>/storage/muninn.database)
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR
Never prompt; missing values are errors
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_NON_INTERACTIVE\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
More output (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print nothing but errors and requested data
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_QUIET\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to every confirmation
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_YES\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\-mode\fR \fI<THEME_MODE>\fR
Override the theme mode for this run
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
light
.IP \(bu 2
dark
.IP \(bu 2
system
.RE
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_THEME_MODE\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH NOTES
.PP
Your user identity is a signing key of its own, made on first use and named after the current
author. It certifies each node that acts for you; peers check those certificates, so a phone or
a second computer is recognised as you. Linking: on the new node run `mnnn user link TARGET`
(an address, peer or invite link of one of your nodes), then approve it there with
`mnnn user approve`. A revoked node stays revoked; peers learn it the next time they talk.
See `mnnn help users`.
.PP
Examples:
  mnnn user show
  mnnn user label "Ada Lovelace"
  mnnn user link muninn://join/…
  mnnn user approve phone
  mnnn user nodes
  mnnn user revoke phone

.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
mnnn\-user\-show(1)
Your user: id, name and linked nodes
.TP
mnnn\-user\-list(1)
The users this node knows (you, connections, org members, reviewers)
.TP
mnnn\-user\-label(1)
Rename your user (peers see it the next time they talk)
.TP
mnnn\-user\-nodes(1)
The nodes linked to a user (default: you), revoked ones too
.TP
mnnn\-user\-link(1)
Link this node to the user of another node of yours (address, peer, name or invite link); that node\*(Aqs owner approves it
.TP
mnnn\-user\-approve(1)
Approve a node asking to link to you (default: the only request)
.TP
mnnn\-user\-revoke(1)
Revoke one of your nodes (peer id, its end, or label); it no longer acts for you
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
